### run 5
===========================================================================
 SPARSE STEREO, BLOCK MATCHING AND DESCRIPTOR MATCHING -- device arms
===========================================================================
 device: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3070 Ti (sm_86, 48 SMs, 608 GB/s peak)
 OpenCV 4.5.4 -- cv::cuda role arms COMPILED IN
===========================================================================
 THE DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE ANY OF THE NUMBERS BELOW WERE TAKEN
===========================================================================
 Per case, because there is no project-wide bar and inventing one is
 forbidden. Where a threshold is a judgement nobody has made it is a
 STOP AND ASK and is left empty rather than filled in.

 THE ONE MAGNITUDE EVERY CASE SHARES, and where it comes from: a difference
 smaller than the larger of the two arms' printed sample RANGES is not a
 difference. That is derived from the measurement's own noise rather than
 chosen -- every pair below goes through timeKernelPaired, which brackets
 both arms inside every round and alternates their order, and prints whether
 the two ranges are disjoint.

 METRICS: (1) kernel-resident median with its spread, both sides of every
 pair on ONE EXPLICIT STREAM; (2) device working set -- allocation sum for
 binCV against binCV, cudaMemGetInfo delta over 32 replicas for anything
 crossing to OpenCV; (3) accuracy, PUBLISHED but not decided on for the two
 ops whose output differs from their bar. Code size and portability decide
 nothing here.

 CASE A -- the brute-force matcher against cv::cuda's BFMatcher.
   Arms: binCV's tiled matcher; knnMatchAsync(k=2) on CV_8U descriptors (what
   cv::cuda::ORB emits, and the path whose HammingDist::reduceIter issues one
   __popc per BYTE -- 32 per 256-bit descriptor); and knnMatchAsync on CV_32S
   over the IDENTICAL bytes, where matchHamming_gpu<int> issues eight. The
   CV_32S arm is the BAR, because the bar is the best existing option, not
   the worst; CV_8U is reported beside it as what the default path costs.
   TIMED REGION: knnMatchAsync plus a committed device-side ratio kernel on
   the same stream. knnMatchConvert is a HOST download and is OUTSIDE the
   event window on every arm -- timing it would compare binCV's resident
   kernel against an OpenCV round trip.
   DECIDES AT 5000 x 5000. The pipeline point 470 x 470 is reported and does
   NOT decide, because both arms are predicted to sit on the launch floor
   there -- which is why the floor is printed next to every arm.
   SHIPS as the recommended device arm if, at map scale, binCV is not slower
   than the CV_32S arm by more than the larger printed range AND its working
   set is not larger. Otherwise it does not ship as a recommendation: the two
   working sets are the same arrays, so no memory-side argument is available
   in either direction.
   PREDICTION: binCV ~= CV_32S within the spread; ~4x ahead of CV_8U; memory
   a wash.

 CASE B -- is the gate still the right answer on device? (issue #61's own
 question). Four arms on identical inputs: device brute force, device gated,
 host brute force, host gated. TWO-SIDED:
   * SPEED: the gate's speed rationale survives only if the device gated arm
     beats the device brute-force arm by more than both printed ranges at the
     pipeline point. PREDICTION: it does not -- the whole match there is a
     launch's worth of work.
   * MEMORY: arithmetic, not a measurement. The gated arm carries two
     position arrays (and two octave arrays) the ungated one does not. Under
     'memory wins' that counts AGAINST the gate on device, and this rule says
     so before the numbers arrive.
   * ACCURACY: untouched by the device and not re-measured here. The device
     computes the same admitted set bit for bit; the suite pins it.
   EXPECTED FINDING, pre-registered: on device the gate is an ACCURACY
   feature and not a speed feature. If the measurement contradicts this it is
   reported as a finding, not smoothed.
   WHAT IT COVERS: a kernel-resident microbenchmark. The number that would
   decide ADOPTION is the match stage's share of an end-to-end pipeline; no
   committed report records it and no resident device pipeline calls these
   kernels. That is a deferral, not a number to invent.

 CASE C -- sparse stereo. cv::cuda has NO sparse stereo API (cudastereo's
 four entries are all dense), so ruling R2 would make the speed verdict
 OUTSTANDING -- but a stronger bar exists and is used instead, because the
 bar is the best existing option a caller actually has: binCV's own device
 denseDisparityBinary on the same frame, then index the map at the keypoints,
 recorded in docs/reports/cuda.md at 0.39 ms and 442 KB.
   SHIPS as the recommended keypoint-shaped stereo path if coarse + refine
   together are below 0.39 ms AND the working set is below 442 KB. Both are
   recorded measurements from this repo, not judgements. If sparse is not
   faster, that is the finding and the recommendation becomes 'run the dense
   kernel and index it'.
   ACCURACY published beside the number and not decided on: the sub-pixel
   residual against a pair of KNOWN disparity.

 CASE D -- block matching against cv::cuda::SparsePyrLKOpticalFlow. ROLE
 ONLY: route (b)'s algorithm on bytes against route (a)'s on bits, and
 correctness is settled against the HOST binCV kernel, never against OpenCV.
   (i) FOOTPRINT, derived and falsifiable, with no threshold to choose: two
       binary pyramids are exactly 1/8 the bytes of two 8-bit ones, and the
       points, the status bytes and the scratch are added to give a COMPUTED
       ratio. The condition is that the MEASURED cudaMemGetInfo ratio
       reproduces the computed one within the meter's own measured step. If
       it does not, the footprint claim does not rest on the format and that
       is the finding. (A '4x' bar was proposed for this case and is DELETED:
       8x is derived from bits per byte, the word 'half' came from nobody.)
   (ii) SPEED: not slower by more than the larger printed range.
   (iii) ACCURACY: reported, never decided on here -- the magnitude is a
       judgement nobody has made. Case D produces numbers and a
       recommendation, not a ship.

 CASE E -- the fast-arm gate check, with the excluding input NAMED per op, so
 the ~1.00x control is runnable rather than asserted:
   * the matchers and the coarse stereo stage: a descriptor wider than
     impl::kMatchTileMaxWords words, which the shared-memory staging refuses;
   * stereo refinement and block matching: a window impl::windowSpanFits
     rejects.
 Anything other than ~1.00x there means the switch is not switching.

 STOP AND ASK -- thresholds nobody has set, left empty on purpose:
   1. CASE D's accuracy floor: how much tracking yield may route (a) give up
      against cv::cuda LK and still be worth recommending? Case D is
      ship-blocked on it.
   2. CASE C's sub-pixel residual floor: the residual is published; what
      residual would make the sparse arm not worth running is unset.
   3. CASE A's fallback: if binCV lands slower than the CV_32S arm, does the
      op ship for residency's sake with the gap documented, or is 'cv::cuda's
      matcher is the better option for a caller who already has OpenCV' the
      honest answer? The census entry was rescued by a memory argument; this
      one has none.
