   [meter 2: cudaMemGetInfo] binCV ungated, per working set        0.03 MB
   [meter 2: its own STEP  ] this driver reserves in 2.00 MB units,
                             measured right here by allocating one byte at a
                             time until the reading moved.
                             This working set fits inside ONE unit, so the
                             reading above is the meter's RESOLUTION and not
                             a footprint: the identical arrays read 0.00 MB
                             or 2.00 MB depending only on how much of the
                             current unit the previous allocation left over.
                             Meter 1 is what measures this working set.
                             Never divide one meter by the other.
   [meter 2: cudaMemGetInfo] cv::cuda BFMatcher, per working set      0.50 MB
   [meter 2: its own STEP  ] this driver reserves in 2.00 MB units,
                             measured right here by allocating one byte at a
                             time until the reading moved.
                             This working set fits inside ONE unit, so the
                             reading above is the meter's RESOLUTION and not
                             a footprint: the identical arrays read 0.00 MB
                             or 2.00 MB depending only on how much of the
                             current unit the previous allocation left over.
                             Meter 1 is what measures this working set.
                             Never divide one meter by the other.
   OpenCV's reading is an UPPER bound: GpuMat may pool, and the
   metered scope includes one knnMatchAsync so the match buffer it
   creates is counted -- as binCV's record array is.
   THE GAP IS THE ROW PITCH, and that part is arithmetic rather than
   allocator behaviour: a 256-bit descriptor is a 32-BYTE ROW, and
   GpuMat pitches a row to a 512-byte multiple, so OpenCV's two
   descriptor matrices carry ~16x their own data. A packed device
   array does not. The design this family came from computed both
   sides UNPITCHED and predicted a wash; the measurement says
   otherwise, and the prediction is what was wrong.
   [meter 1: allocation sum ] binCV descriptors + records (meter 1)      36.7 KB
