 ACCURACY, published beside the numbers and NOT decided on:
   500 of 500 keypoints matched; mean |disparity - 21| = 0.0145 px,
   worst 0.0556 px, against a pair whose true disparity is exactly 21.
   500 landed within half a pixel; 0 landed at the SCAN'S EDGE
   (|error| >= refineRadius = 4).
   THE EDGE COUNT IS THE INTERESTING NUMBER AND IT IS NOT A DEVICE
   RESULT: the suite holds this kernel bit-exact against the host,
   so whatever it does here the host does too. It is a property of a
   ONE-BIT window refinement -- the frames are packed by a single
   threshold, and where the local binary window is uniform EVERY
   disparity scores zero, so the tie rule takes the smallest and the
   result pins to the scan's low edge. A FAST corner in the wide
   image is not a corner in the thresholded one. Reported rather
   than tuned away: the fix is a richer packing (the N-bit or census
   path), not a change to this kernel.
   The dense path this is measured against returns an INTEGER map,
   so the sub-pixel output is the reason to run the sparse arm at
   all. What residual would make it not worth running is STOP AND
   ASK 2.

